# Every agent, not only this one

These rules apply whenever an AI agent changes how AI agents work: their
instructions, rules, skills, subagents, hooks, permissions, MCP servers,
plugins or settings, in the dotfiles, in dyai, or in any other repository.

- This machine runs seven agents from one dyai config: Claude Code, Codex,
  Gemini, opencode, Copilot, Antigravity and Hermes. A change meant for the
  agent you are is a change for all seven, unless it is specific to one.
- Before editing, list where each of them keeps the thing being changed, and
  change every one of them in the same task. Search next to the code you
  touch for the others:
  `rg -i 'claude|codex|gemini|opencode|copilot|antigravity|agy|hermes'`.
- Prefer the one place they all read: an instruction goes in a partial under
  `home/.chezmoitemplates/ai/`, a permission in the dyai policy
  (`ai/dyai-data.yaml.tmpl`), an extension in `~/.config/dyai/config.yaml`,
  rather than in one agent's own file.
- In the dotfiles, each agent's own files live in `home/private_dot_claude/`,
  `private_dot_codex/`, `private_dot_gemini/` (Gemini and Antigravity),
  `private_dot_config/opencode/`, `private_dot_copilot/` and
  `private_dot_hermes/`. In dyai, a feature has one function per agent in
  `lib/` and a test for each.
- When a change cannot reach some agents (no such hook, a matcher that
  cannot express the rule), say in the reply which ones are left out and why.
